    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Living in a rental often means compromising on aesthetics, and perhaps the most glaring compromise is the flooring. Whether it’s 1970s avocado-green ceramic, cracked lino or beige tiles that have seen better decades, an ugly floor can dampen the spirit of even the most beautifully decorated room. Since you can’t renovate permanently, you need solutions that are high-impact, low-cost and, most importantly, designed to be removable.</p>
<p>Covering your floors doesn’t just improve the look of your home; it can also protect the original surface, which may help when it comes to getting your deposit back. From modern vinyl to classic textiles, there are plenty of ways to transform your space without breaking your tenancy agreement.</p>
<p>Before you start, read that agreement: some say no adhesive products on floors. If in doubt, ask your landlord or letting agent and get their answer in writing. Photograph the tiles alongside your check-in inventory, and test any adhesive product on a hidden tile, such as behind the toilet or under a unit, for a week or two of normal use before covering a whole floor. Adhesive can lift old glazes, loose grout or painted finishes, so check the test patch carefully when you peel it off. If you want to avoid adhesive altogether, our guide to <a href="/blog/cover-ugly-rental-tiles-no-drilling">covering ugly rental tiles without drilling</a> goes deeper into the no-fixings options.</p>

<h2 id="vinyl-tiles">1. Vinyl tiles</h2>
<p>Peel-and-stick vinyl tiles are one of the most popular choices for rental transformations. Modern versions have come a long way from the thin, papery stickers of the past: today’s options feature realistic textures, matt finishes and durable wear layers, and are designed to mimic marble, slate, cement or intricate encaustic patterns.</p>
<p>The key to success with vinyl tiles is preparation. The existing tile must be scrubbed clean of any grease or wax, or the adhesive won’t bond. If your rental tiles have deep grout lines, you may find the vinyl “telegraphs” (shows the indentation) over time. Thicker, rigid vinyl tiles bridge grout lines better; filling the grout is a change to the property, so ask permission before using any filler. Vinyl tiles are water-resistant, which makes them a good candidate for a kitchen or bathroom floor, but keep them out of the shower itself.</p>
<p>When it’s time to move out, warming the tiles with a hairdryer on a low setting softens the glue, so they should peel off — but results vary with the product, the age of the tile and how long the vinyl has been down, which is why you test first.</p>

<h2 id="large-rugs">2. Large rugs</h2>
<p>If the thought of sticking anything to the floor makes you nervous, the “wall-to-wall” rug method is your best friend. By choosing a rug about 30–45 cm (12–18 in) smaller than the room in each direction, you mask most of the ugly tile while adding warmth and softening sound. Our guide to <a href="/blog/rug-size-for-12x14-living-room-with-sectional">rug sizes for a 3.7 × 4.3 m (12 × 14 ft) living room</a> shows how to size a large rug around your furniture.</p>
<p>For high-traffic areas such as living rooms, look for flat-weave rugs or indoor-outdoor varieties. These are easier to clean and trap less dust than a high-pile shag. If you’re covering a kitchen floor, consider a heavy-duty vinyl floor cloth: these are thin, lie flat and feature printed designs that look like patterned tile work. They don’t need adhesive, relying on their weight and a non-slip backing to stay in place. Be aware that some rubber or latex backings can discolour certain floor finishes over time, so test in a corner, and lift and air rugs regularly so moisture doesn’t get trapped underneath. A suitable rug pad also stops rugs sliding on slick tile; see our guide to <a href="/blog/rug-pad-types-for-hardwood-floors">rug pad types</a>.</p>
<p>Layering rugs — placing a smaller, more colourful rug over a large, neutral jute or sisal base — is another styling trick that draws the eye away from the slivers of tile still visible at the edges.</p>

<h2 id="foam-mats">3. Foam mats</h2>
<p>Forget the primary-coloured alphabet mats you see in playrooms. Modern interlocking foam tiles now come in more sophisticated patterns, including faux wood, marble and concrete. They are popular with renters because they create a “floating” floor that sits on the original tile without adhesive.</p>
<p>Foam mats are particularly useful in rooms where you stand a lot, such as a home gym or a laundry area, because they offer good cushioning. They are easy to install: snap the puzzle-like edges together and trim the borders with a utility knife to fit the walls. Because they are thick, they also insulate, so cold tile feels warmer underfoot in winter. Keep them away from heaters and open flames, and choose products that meet the relevant fire-safety standards. Since they aren’t glued down, there’s no adhesive, so less risk of residue, although some mat materials can mark floor finishes, so test a corner and lift them occasionally to let the floor beneath dry. When you move, you can pack them up and take them to your next home.</p>

<h2 id="click-lock-planks">4. Click-lock planks</h2>
<p>If you’re willing to invest a bit more for a truly transformative look, click-lock luxury vinyl planks (LVP) or laminate are the premium choice. This is a floating floor system where the planks lock into one another rather than being glued down, so the tile underneath is left untouched.</p>
<p>Click-lock flooring can bridge small irregularities and grout lines, although floating floors need a reasonably flat surface, so check the maker’s guidance on how much unevenness the product tolerates. Leave the expansion gap they specify around the edges of the room, and don’t nail anything into the floor or skirting. The result feels and sounds much more like a permanent floor. While this is the most labour-intensive and expensive of the temporary options, it makes the biggest difference to daily life. Before installing, lay a thin foam underlayment: it protects the original tile from friction scratches and adds an extra layer of moisture protection. When you leave, you simply un-click the floor, stack the planks and take them to your next home.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Measure the clearance under every door the new floor passes before you buy. Don’t plane or trim doors yourself: that’s a change to the property, so ask your landlord first and get their permission in writing.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="floor-decals">5. Floor decals</h2>
<p>If your tiles aren’t ugly in texture but just a boring colour, floor decals or “tile tattoos” are a precise fix. These are individual stickers cut to the size of your existing tiles. You can cover every tile or create a random pattern by covering only every third or fourth one.</p>
<p>Decals are usually made of floor-grade vinyl with a slip-resistant laminate coating. They are much thinner than full vinyl tiles, so they are less likely to create a trip hazard at the door frame. This is often the most budget-friendly way to add a designer feel to a small space such as a cloakroom or an entryway. Because you aren’t covering the grout, the original layout of the tile stays visible, just with a much-improved colour scheme. Decals cut slightly smaller than the tile stick to the smooth glaze rather than porous grout and are easier to remove later — as with any adhesive product, patch test on a hidden tile first.</p>

<h2 id="natural-mats">6. Natural mats</h2>
<p>Where you want an organic, calm look, bamboo or seagrass mats make excellent temporary covers. Bamboo slat rugs are backed with felt or rubber, which stops them sliding on slick tile (check that a rubber backing won’t mark your floor). They offer a hard surface that is easy to wipe down, making them suitable for kitchens or even enclosed balconies, though they shouldn’t be left sitting in water.</p>
<p>Seagrass and sisal are very durable and can withstand heavy foot traffic. These natural fibres bring a texture that distracts from the cold, clinical feel of old tile. While they aren’t a wall-to-wall solution, a well-placed large bamboo mat can cover most of the room, shifting the focus from the floor’s imperfections to the natural beauty of the wood or fibre.</p>

<h2 id="canvas-cloths">7. Canvas cloths</h2>
<p>A traditional yet often overlooked method is the floor cloth. Historically, these were heavy pieces of canvas that were painted, stencilled and then sealed with several layers of varnish to make them stiff and water-resistant. Today, they make a satisfying DIY project for the creative renter.</p>
<p>You can buy pre-primed canvas, paint it to match your decor — perhaps a faux chequered marble or a modern abstract design — and seal it with a water-based polyurethane. The result is a custom-sized, durable, wipeable floor covering that sits flat on the tile. Because they are stiff, they don’t bunch up like rugs, though a non-slip underlay is still sensible on smooth tile, and because they are hand-painted, each one is unique to your home. They are particularly effective in dining areas where food spills are common, as they can be wiped clean just like the tile underneath. When you move, roll them painted side out to avoid cracking.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>An ugly rental floor doesn’t have to define your home. Start with the least risky options — large rugs, floor cloths and floating floors that need no adhesive — and use peel-and-stick products only after a patch test and, where your tenancy agreement is unclear, your landlord’s written permission. Prepare the surface well, check door clearances and keep your check-in photos, and you can enjoy a floor you love now and leave the original tile as you found it.</p>

<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="sticky-residue">Will peel-and-stick tiles leave a sticky residue when I remove them?</h3>
<p>Many good-quality vinyl tiles use a pressure-sensitive adhesive designed to stay on the back of the vinyl rather than the floor, but results vary. If some residue remains, it can often be removed with warm soapy water or a citrus-based adhesive remover suitable for tiles and grout. Always test a small, hidden area first.</p>
<h3 id="bathroom-humidity">Can I use these solutions in a bathroom with high humidity?</h3>
<p>Yes, but choose the right material. Vinyl tiles and click-lock vinyl planks rated for wet areas are good choices for bathroom floors, but don’t use vinyl inside the shower, where water trapped behind it can lead to mould. Rugs and foam mats can trap moisture underneath, which may lead to mould or mildew on the grout, so keep the room well ventilated and lift the covering regularly to let the floor dry.</p>
<h3 id="height-at-the-door">How do I handle the height difference at the door?</h3>
<p>Thicker materials such as click-lock planks or foam mats raise the floor level. A transition strip (a sloped edge piece) at the doorway reduces the trip risk; in a rental, choose a removable strip that doesn’t need screws or glue, and check with your landlord before fitting it. Always check that your door has enough clearance to swing open over the new flooring before you start.</p>
<h3 id="cheapest-option">What is the cheapest way to cover a large tiled area?</h3>
<p>Large area rugs or indoor-outdoor rugs are usually the most cost-effective way to cover a big floor. If you enjoy DIY, painting a large canvas floor cloth is also very budget-friendly.</p>
<h3 id="fill-grout-lines">Do I need to fill the grout lines before putting vinyl over them?</h3>
<p>If the grout lines are very deep or wide, thin vinyl may eventually sink into them, creating visible lines on the surface. Filling them is a change to the property, so ask your landlord before using grout tape or any filler. Alternatively, a thicker, rigid-core vinyl plank is usually stiff enough to bridge the gaps without filling.</p>

